@page "/exampleformasync"
@using HelmerDemo.BlazorServerTwo.Application.Features.ExampleForm.AsyncCommit.Application.Domain
<h3>Decoupled persons form</h3>

<p>In this architecture, the frontend is decoupled from the backend. Any submit is async committed to the backend, and persisted. There is no synchronisation. There are clear onion layers between Blazor, Application and Frontend.</p>
<p>Advantages</p>
<ul>
    <li>Decoupling: The Domain model, Database model or blazor frontend can be changed without affecting one another.</li>
    <li>Async commit: The user can continue to use the application while the backend is persisting the data.</li>
    <li>Flexibility: The architecture allows for more flexible changes and adaptations.</li>
</ul>
<p>Drawbacks</p>
<ul>
    <li>There is no synchronization between Blazor sessions. (Is that a drawback or an advantage?)</li>
    <li>There is a lot of code, due to all the adapters and contracts! This could be mistaken for complexity.</li>
    <li>Validation logic is both in Frontend and in Backend.</li>
    <li>Adding a property to a contract, means the FrontEnd does not break. There is no hard coupling. So how do I know what to fix in the Frontend?</li>
    <li>Latency: The user will not see the new person in the list until the async commit is complete.</li>
</ul>

<h4>People</h4>

<p>List of all people entered:</p>
<ol>
@foreach(var person in ExampleFormViewState.People)
{
    <li>@person.FullName</li>
}
</ol>

<br />
<h4>Add Person</h4>
<EditForm Model="@ExampleFormViewState.AddPersonViewState" OnValidSubmit="AddPersonFormSubmitAsync">
    <DataAnnotationsValidator />
    <ValidationSummary />
    <div>
        <label>First Name:</label>
        <InputText @bind-Value="ExampleFormViewState.AddPersonViewState.FirstName" />
    </div>
    <div>
        <label>Last Name:</label>
        <InputText @bind-Value="ExampleFormViewState.AddPersonViewState.LastName" />
    </div>
    <div>
        <label>Gender:</label>
        <InputSelect @bind-Value="ExampleFormViewState.AddPersonViewState.Gender">
            @foreach (var gender in Enum.GetValues(typeof(Gender)))
            {
                <option value="@gender">@gender</option>
            }
        </InputSelect>
    </div>
    <button type="submit">Add Person</button>
</EditForm>